     5	# Design analysis for the D6 variant
     6	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
     7	#    - Input still contains two kinds of structures: one edge palette (2xN / Nx2) and several hollow sprites.
     8	#    - Original output kept the full canvas: palette stayed on the edge, sprites stayed in place, only mapped holes changed
     9	#      from bg to their fill colors.
    10	#    - New output discards the whole outer canvas and the palette. After filling the eligible holes, we crop to the minimal
    11	#      rectangle containing every sprite cell. Example: if a top palette occupies rows 0-1 and two sprites live in rows 4-8,
    12	#      original logic returned the full HxW solved grid; the variant returns only rows/cols spanning those sprites.
    13	#    - The rule change therefore lives after filling, at the "what region is returned" stage, not in palette reading or hole filling.
    14	# b) Complexity calibration
    15	#    - We keep 2-4 sprites, 2-5 palette entries, and one distractor sprite. This preserves the original ambiguity pressure:
    16	#      the solver must still infer the palette and decide which holes are fillable.
    17	#    - To make D6 visible, generation now guarantees that sprites do not touch the full-grid border. This prevents the crop from
    18	#      silently degenerating to the original canvas size.
    19	# c) Implicit-assumption review of the original main()
    20	#    - _bg_boundary() assumes the outer border is mostly bg. This still holds because the palette remains small and sprites are
    21	#      generated away from the grid boundary.
    22	#    - _best_palette() assumes a single isolated edge palette whose outer stripe provides unique border keys. Unchanged.
    23	#    - _fill_enclosed_bg_regions() treats a bg component as a hole iff it does not touch the full-grid boundary. This remains valid
    24	#      because all sprite holes stay strictly internal to the full grid.
    25	#    - The old "return out" relied on output size being fixed. That assumption is exactly what D6 breaks, so the new main() must
    26	#      crop after filling and must exclude the palette from the crop mask.
    27	# d) Placement legality
    28	#    - Sprites still cannot overlap each other or the palette, and still keep a 1-cell bg moat between objects.
    29	#    - New generator constraint: the entire sprite bbox must lie at least 1 cell inside the full grid boundary. This guarantees a
    30	#      proper crop margin while preserving solver assumptions about enclosed holes.
    31	# e) Visual observability
    32	#    - From examples, a human can see that the palette disappears in the output and the remaining picture is a tight window around
    33	#      the sprites. The crop boundary is therefore a visible, structural change rather than a hidden metadata change.
    34	#    - The crop is determined by spatial extent only, never by color ids or sorting by numeric color values.
    35	# f) Visual cleanliness
    36	#    - We avoid border-touching sprites, so the cropped output is a compact cluster of sprites without unrelated empty frame area.
    37	#    - The palette stays isolated in the input, which keeps the "legend" visually separate from the sprite field.
    38	# g) Color-change inference
